            <ArticleTitle>Giant Renal Angiomyolipoma Following Ovarian Stimulation Therapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0090-4295(17)31182-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.urology.2017.10.039</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Renal angiomyolipomas (AMLs) are benign tumors with higher prevalence in women. Female hormones have been shown to induce AML enlargement. This case refers to a 40-year-old woman with 4 left kidney AMLs, the larger ones with 1.0 and 1.3 cm. Ten months after ovarian stimulation for egg harvesting, a computed tomography revealed an 18-cm AML with large-caliber vessels. Given her high risk of AML bleeding, the patient was submitted to selective arterial embolization, which turned out unsuccessful, supporting a plan of nephron-sparing surgery. Our case highlights the pro-growth effects of female hormones on AML, with particular emphasis to ovarian stimulation.</AbstractText>
